<article class="env-definition" id="06GC">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06GC">Definition <span data-tag="06GC">90.3.1</span>.</a> Let $\Lambda $ be a Noetherian ring and let $\Lambda \to k$ be a finite ring map where $k$ is a field. We define <em>$\mathcal{C}_\Lambda $</em> to be the category with </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
objects are pairs $(A, \varphi )$ where $A$ is an Artinian local $\Lambda $-algebra and where $\varphi : A/\mathfrak m_ A \to k$ is a $\Lambda $-algebra isomorphism, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
morphisms $f : (B, \psi ) \to (A, \varphi )$ are local $\Lambda $-algebra homomorphisms such that $\varphi \circ (f \bmod \mathfrak m) = \psi $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> We say we are in the <em>classical case</em> if $\Lambda $ is a Noetherian complete local ring and $k$ is its residue field. </p>
</article>